WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:02.607
These are common classification traps.

00:00:02.607 --> 00:00:07.227
The visual appearance of a value can tempt us to classify too quickly.

00:00:07.227 --> 00:00:10.262
ZIP codes and patient IDs are labels.

00:00:10.262 --> 00:00:14.567
Satisfaction is ordered but not necessarily equally spaced.

00:00:14.567 --> 00:00:21.177
Heart rate may be stored as an integer while representing a measurement.

00:00:21.177 --> 00:00:22.032
ER visits is a count.

00:00:22.032 --> 00:00:24.877
Celsius temperature is interval rather than ratio.

00:00:24.877 --> 00:00:31.637
When in doubt, describe the variable in a sentence and ask what operations make real-world sense.

00:00:31.637 --> 00:00:37.567
Connect this idea to an e-commerce team preparing customer, purchase, and web-event data.

00:00:37.567 --> 00:00:43.067
Ask what could go wrong if the concept were misunderstood: could rows be duplicated,

00:00:43.067 --> 00:00:50.997
important records disappear, categories be interpreted incorrectly, or a report become difficult to reproduce?

00:00:50.997 --> 00:00:56.802
A strong analyst can explain both the SQL operation and the assumption behind it.

00:00:56.802 --> 00:01:02.754
After the example, summarize the idea in one plain-language sentence before moving on.
